## SECTION 4 — DISPOSABLE CAPSULE-NOZZLE DISPENSING SYSTEM

This section is the technical core of the ToastFlow AI patent position and the primary engineering differentiation.

### 4.1 Capsule Architecture

Physical form:
Outer body: Food-grade polypropylene (PP) cylinder, 30mm outer diameter × 55mm length
Piston: Food-grade silicone rubber plunger (shore A 40), slidably sealed against body inner wall, zero leakage at up to 150N axial force
Product chamber: Contains 20mL of spreadable food material (yields 5–12g per dispense depending on spread density)
Fill end seal: Heat-sealed aluminium foil membrane (pierceable by appliance piercing crown), nitrogen-flushed headspace for oxidation-free shelf life

Outlet and spreading lip (the key innovation):
The capsule base end tapers to a PTFE outlet tube of 2.5mm orifice diameter. Surrounding and extending beyond this orifice is a flexible spreading lip manufactured from medical-grade LSR (Liquid Silicone Rubber, food-safe, FDA 21 CFR 177.2600 compliant). The lip profile varies by spread type — this is a product line variant, not a user-configurable parameter:

- Straight thin lip (2mm extension, 18mm width): For honey and low-viscosity spreads (Class A, 1,000–10,000 mPa·s). Minimizes drag, prevents pulling surface crumbs.
- Grooved lip (3mm extension, 22mm width, three 0.8mm longitudinal channels): For jam and medium-viscosity spreads with particulates (Class B, 10,000–50,000 mPa·s). Channels distribute extrudate laterally during traverse.
- Tapered wide lip (4mm extension, 28mm width, tapered wedge geometry): For peanut butter, almond butter, and pistachio cream (Class C, 50,000–250,000 mPa·s). Wider contact area distributes force and prevents bread tearing under high-viscosity paste.

The lip must survive one complete spreading pass without tearing or permanent deformation. Material qualification: Medical-grade LSR validated at 1,000-cycle abrasion simulations against standard toast surface textures.

Mechanical drive interface:
A gear rack of module 1.0 teeth is molded into the capsule's outer surface along a 40mm axial length. This rack engages the carriage's pinion gear to translate the piston. In an alternative embodiment (cost-reduced, lower precision): a threaded external collar replaces the rack, driven by a rotating nut on the carriage.

RFID tag:
An RC522-compatible passive RFID tag (13.56MHz) is affixed to the capsule base. Tag data fields: spread type identifier, viscosity class, allergen flags (top 8 allergens, binary), manufacturing date, batch number, expiry date, and recommended carriage speed and pre-warm temperature.

Shelf life: 12 months ambient (nitrogen-flushed + foil seal), 18 months refrigerated. For Class A spreads (honey) ambient shelf life may exceed 24 months due to natural antimicrobial properties.

Color coding (cap color standard):
Gold: Honey
Green: Pistachio cream
Red: Strawberry jam
Dark brown: Chocolate spread
Beige: Peanut butter
Purple: Blueberry jam
White: Cream cheese (refrigerated SKU only)
Orange: Apricot jam

Cost per capsule: $0.12–$0.20 at pilot scale (50k units/year), declining to $0.06–$0.09 at volume production (5M units/year).

### 4.2 Linear Carriage System

The carriage is the sole motorized element of the dispensing subsystem. Its design is derived from the single-motor-per-axis principle, minimizing BOM and failure modes.

Rail: 8mm diameter hardened steel linear rail, 200mm total length, mounted above the bread slot on the appliance's upper chassis. The rail spans across both slots (system accommodates 1 or 2 simultaneous slices).

Drive: NEMA 8 stepper motor (12V, 200 steps/revolution, 1.8°/step) driving a GT2 timing belt (2mm pitch, 6mm width). Belt connects to carriage shuttle. Total linear travel: 180mm (covers 160mm bread width with 10mm margin each side).

Step resolution with 1/16 microstepping: 0.019mm/step — sufficient for precise extrusion control.

Carriage shuttle: Houses the capsule receiver socket (30mm inner diameter, spring-loaded clamping collar), the piercing crown (stainless steel 304, 3-prong geometry, fixed position), the pinion gear (module 1.0, 12 teeth, driven via bevel gear pair from the same motor shaft using a small geartrain), and the RC522 RFID reader coil.

The mechanical linkage between carriage translation and piston advancement: The motor shaft drives the belt for carriage translation via a primary gear set, and simultaneously drives the pinion gear (which engages the capsule rack) via a secondary gear reduction at the carriage. The gear ratio is set so that for every 1mm of carriage travel, the piston advances 0.11mm, extruding approximately 0.077mL per mm of travel. Over a full 160mm spreading pass: ~12mL dispensed. For a single light application (~6mL): half-pass or reduced piston coupling engagement via a software-controlled slip clutch (electromagnetic, 5V coil).

The key property: spread volume is geometrically guaranteed proportional to carriage travel distance. No flow sensor, no mass flow controller, and no feedback loop is required for uniform coverage. This is the fundamental elegance of the linked-motion architecture.

Maximum piston force: The stepper motor at rated torque (22 N·cm) through the gear reduction produces approximately 85N axial piston force. At 35°C pre-warm temperature, Class C peanut butter viscosity drops from ~250,000 mPa·s to ~80,000 mPa·s and the required extrusion force is within the 85N rating. For cold start without pre-warm, Class C materials may stall the motor — see Engineering Risk 1 in Section 12.

Carriage speed: Software-adjustable per viscosity class.
Class A (honey): 80mm/s traverse — fast pass, thin layer
Class B (jam): 55mm/s — moderate speed, allows grooved lip to distribute
Class C (peanut butter): 30mm/s — slow pass, maximum force available, lip pressure highest

Spreading pattern: 3-pass serpentine, covering a 160mm × 90mm nominal bread surface. Total dispense time: approximately 7–9 seconds including direction reversals.

### 4.3 Anti-Drip System

At the end of the final spreading pass, the stepper motor is commanded to reverse direction by a precise step count corresponding to 0.3mm of piston retraction. This action pulls spread material back from the orifice tip, preventing passive drip during carriage return. The retraction distance is optimized per viscosity class (Class A: 0.5mm; Class B: 0.3mm; Class C: 0.15mm — larger retraction for lower viscosity materials).

### 4.4 Wiper and Capsule Ejection

During carriage return to home position, the capsule's spreading lip passes over a stationary silicone wiper blade (Shore A 30 LSR, 3mm thickness, 35mm width) mounted on the appliance body adjacent to the bread slot. The wiper removes residual surface spread from the lip without contacting any permanent appliance surface directly.

At the home position (or a dedicated ejection station 10mm past home), the clamping collar is electrically released, and the capsule falls by gravity into a removable spent capsule bin tray. The bin tray holds up to 10 spent capsules before requiring emptying. It slides out from the front of the appliance and is dishwasher-safe.

### 4.5 Capsule Carousel Supply System

The rotary carousel holds 4 capsules in indexed positions. A 5V micro-servo (180° rotation, 1.5 kg·cm torque) rotates the carousel to bring the selected capsule to the pick-up station.

Selection logic: User selects spread type on touchscreen or via app. MCU looks up which carousel slot holds a capsule with the matching RFID type (read during carousel load by a secondary fixed RFID reader at each slot). Carousel indexes to that position. Carriage moves to pick-up station, receiver engages capsule, clamping collar locks, piercing crown punctures foil seal, carriage moves to spreading position.

If a selected spread type is not loaded, the display shows "Load [spread name] capsule — Slot [N] empty" and the cycle pauses until the user loads one.

### 4.6 Temperature Conditioning

A PTC (Positive Temperature Coefficient) ceramic heater element (5W, self-regulating, setpoint 35°C) is integrated into the carousel pick-up station. When a Class C (peanut butter, almond butter) or honey capsule is selected, the MCU activates the PTC heater 90 seconds before dispense cycle begins — long enough to warm the capsule's lower 20mm (nozzle zone) to 35°C. This reduces Class C viscosity from ~250,000 mPa·s at 20°C to approximately 60,000–80,000 mPa·s at 35°C, bringing the required extrusion force below the 85N carriage limit.

Pre-warm notification: The display and app show "Warming [spread]... 87 seconds remaining" with a progress ring. This is a user-facing feature, not just a mechanical delay — it creates anticipation and feels like quality signaling.

### 4.7 Cleaning — Comprehensive Statement

The appliance's permanent internal surfaces are not food-contact surfaces. There is no tubing, pump, valve, or permanent nozzle. The complete food path is: capsule interior → capsule orifice → capsule lip → bread surface. All of these are either discarded (capsule) or the final product (bread).

The only cleaning operations required by the user are:

1. Empty spent capsule bin tray (holds 10 spent capsules, approximately once per two weeks for a daily-use household). Duration: 5 seconds.
2. Remove and rinse crumb tray (triggered by on-screen reminder every 30 cycles). Duration: 20 seconds under tap.
3. Wipe appliance exterior with damp cloth as needed. Duration: 15 seconds.

Total user cleaning burden: under 1 minute per month for daily use. This is the primary consumer-facing value differentiator versus any pump-based competitor.

