## SECTION 6 — AI AND SOFTWARE ARCHITECTURE

### 6.1 On-Device Layer (TFLite Micro on ESP32-S3)

Three neural models run locally with zero cloud dependency for core functions:

Model 1 — Bread Classifier:
Architecture: Lightweight CNN (MobileNetV1-style, heavily quantized to INT8)
Input: 3× TCS34725 readings (R, G, B, Clear, IR per sensor = 15 channels), HX711 weight reading, user-selected mode if any
Output: Softmax over 40 bread type classes + confidence score
Model size on-device: ~45KB
Inference latency: ~12ms at 240MHz
Training: Supervised on 40-class synthetic + lab-photographed dataset, augmented for variable lighting

Model 2 — Browning Time-Series Predictor:
Architecture: Quantized LSTM (2 layers, 32 hidden units)
Input: Rolling 10-second window of optical sensor readings (sampled at 2Hz = 20 timesteps × 15 channels)
Output: Predicted seconds to target browning level + early-stop binary signal
Model size: ~30KB
Purpose: Prevents over-toasting due to residual heat, adapts to each cycle's real-time browning progress. This produces a better result than fixed time-based algorithms because it responds to the actual bread state, not assumed parameters.

Model 3 — Fault Detector:
Architecture: Rule-based + shallow decision tree (no neural network — fast, interpretable)
Inputs monitored:
- Carriage stepper current draw (stall = capsule engagement failure or mechanical obstruction)
- DS18B20 temperature readings outside ±15°C of expected profile (heating element fault)
- HX711 weight change < threshold after carriage dispense cycle (empty capsule not detected by RFID, or dispense failure)
- RFID read failure at capsule engagement (tag absent or damaged)
Outputs: Fault code + human-readable message on display + push notification to app
Response: Motor stop + alarm + user guidance

### 6.2 Cloud AI Layer

Architecture: Mobile app is the AI reasoning layer. Device executes structured commands. Cloud provides personalization, language understanding, and nutritional intelligence.

The cloud LLM (accessed via HTTPS REST API) handles:

User Preference Engine:
Builds a behavioral model per user profile across sessions. After 7 days learns morning routine patterns. Proactively suggests "Your usual?" at typical breakfast time. Learns browning preferences per bread type independently (user may prefer light on brioche and dark on sourdough — these are stored as separate entries, not a single global preference).

Nutritional Tracking:
Per-cycle caloric calculation:
Bread calories = weight from load cell × per-gram calorie density for classified bread type (from embedded nutrition database, 40 entries)
Spread calories = dispense volume (step count × 0.077mL/step) × per-mL calorie density for spread type from RFID tag
Total session nutrition logged to Apple Health / Google Fit via app

Voice Command Processing:
Natural language → structured JSON command. Cloud NLP, result transmitted to device via BLE/Wi-Fi. Examples:
"Light on the honey today" → spread_quantity_ml: 5 (vs. default 9)
"Make my Monday usual" → retrieves stored Monday profile for that user
"Skip the spread, just toast" → dispense: false

Capsule Inventory Management:
App tracks capsule consumption per type. When any type falls to 1 capsule remaining: push notification "Pistachio cream running low — reorder?" with direct link to subscription portal.

JSON command structure (device input format):
{
  "user_id": "string",
  "bread_mode": "string (40 types)",
  "browning_level": "integer 1–9",
  "slot": "integer 1–2 or both",
  "spread_type": "string or null",
  "spread_quantity_ml": "float 3.0–18.0 or null",
  "pre_warm": "boolean",
  "nutritional_target_kcal": "float or null",
  "timestamp": "ISO 8601"
}

