## SECTION 8 — FOOD SAFETY AND REGULATORY

### 8.1 Required Certifications

UL 1026 (Electric Household Cooking and Food Preparation Appliances) — United States mandatory
CE Marking + EN 60335-2-9 (Safety of Household Appliances — Particular Requirements for Toasters) — EU mandatory
FCC Part 15B (Unintentional radiator) + Part 15C (Intentional radiator for Wi-Fi and BLE) — US mandatory
ISED RSS-247 (Canada, if shipped to Canadian market)
FDA 21 CFR 177.2600 compliance: applies to capsule materials (PP body, LSR lip, PTFE tube, silicone piston). The appliance body requires no FDA food-contact certification because it does not contact food.

### 8.2 HACCP Analysis

With the disposable capsule architecture, the HACCP critical control point count is radically reduced versus a pump-based design. Only two critical control points exist at the appliance level:

CCP-1 — Capsule foil seal integrity at time of use: The seal must be visually intact (no bulging, no prior puncture). The piercing crown punctures the foil completely in one motion; a failed puncture (partial pierce) is detected by the RFID reader confirming engagement but the load cell detecting no weight reduction on bread after a completed dispense cycle. MCU triggers Fault Code F-03 (dispense failure) and prompts user to reload.

CCP-2 — Capsule storage temperature (Class B and C materials): Jam and nut butters have water activity values requiring ambient storage ≤25°C. The app includes a capsule storage advisory. The appliance carousel ambient temperature is monitored by one DS18B20 probe; if carousel temperature exceeds 30°C (e.g., appliance placed in direct sunlight), the display shows a capsule storage warning.

All other food safety requirements (contamination prevention, shelf life, allergen labeling) are controlled at the capsule manufacturing and packaging stage — not at the appliance level. This is the fundamental regulatory advantage of the disposable architecture.

### 8.3 Allergen Management

The RC522 RFID reader on the carriage reads allergen flags from the capsule tag. The app stores each household user's allergen profile. On spread selection, the MCU compares capsule allergen flags against the active user's profile. If a conflict is detected, the display and app both show a red-bordered warning: "[Spread Name] contains [Allergen]. Confirm or cancel." The cycle does not proceed until the user explicitly confirms on both the touchscreen and the app (dual-confirm for high-risk situations). This creates a logged record of informed consent, which is both a safety feature and a liability protection mechanism.

### 8.4 Acrylamide Liability Mitigation

Acrylamide formation is a known consequence of high-temperature bread browning. The AI browning controller defaults all new user profiles to Browning Level 5 (medium). To access Levels 8 and 9 (dark and very dark), the user must explicitly enable "Dark Toast Mode" in app Settings, at which point a one-time informational screen is shown explaining acrylamide formation at high browning levels, with a clear "Understand and Enable" button required to proceed. This on-record informed consent is stored in the user profile cloud record and provides a documented liability shield.

