## SECTION 12 — ENGINEERING RISK REGISTER

Risk 1 — Piston stall on cold Class C materials (Critical, High Probability)
Peanut butter at 18°C ambient may require >100N extrusion force, exceeding the carriage motor's 85N capacity.
Mitigation: Mandatory 90-second pre-warm at 35°C (PTC heater) reduces Class C viscosity to within motor spec. App enforces this delay — cycle cannot begin for Class C spreads until pre-warm is confirmed by thermocouple reading ≥33°C. Additionally, capsule inner diameter enlarged to 35mm for Class C capsule variant (increases piston area by 36%, reducing required pressure for same force).
Residual risk: Low after mitigations.

Risk 2 — Spreading lip tear on artisan bread with hard crust (Medium Probability, Medium Impact)
Thick sourdough or baguette may have hard surface crust edges that catch the lip during traverse.
Mitigation: Lip design uses a tapered leading edge (30° approach angle) that rides up over surface irregularities rather than catching. Carriage speed for Class C materials (30mm/s) provides low-impact traversal. User-facing guidance in app: "For very hard-crusted bread, allow toast to cool 15 seconds before spread cycle for best results."
Residual risk: Low.

Risk 3 — Capsule ecosystem lock-in backlash (Medium Probability, High Business Impact)
Consumers and media may react negatively to proprietary capsule format (Nespresso precedent is well-known).
Mitigation: From launch, publish the capsule physical specification (dimensional drawing, material spec, drive interface geometry) as a freely available standard document on the company website. License the standard at zero cost. Communicate this proactively in marketing: "Open Capsule Standard — any manufacturer can produce compatible capsules." This removes the lock-in narrative and differentiates ToastFlow AI from beverage capsule players.
Residual risk: Low if communicated from Day 1.

Risk 4 — Spreading non-uniformity on bread with large surface holes (Sourdough, Ciabatta) (Low Probability, Low Impact)
Open-crumb breads with large holes will absorb spread non-uniformly regardless of nozzle geometry.
Mitigation: This is an inherent property of the bread, not a machine failure. In-app messaging for these bread types: "Spread will absorb into open crumb — this is normal for artisan breads." Sets correct user expectation. Optionally, a "double-pass" mode can be offered that makes two full traversals, partially filling holes on the first pass.
Residual risk: Low — user expectation is the control.

Risk 5 — Capsule oil separation in storage (Peanut Butter, Almond Butter) (Medium Probability, Medium Impact)
Natural nut butters separate over weeks in sealed capsules, with oil pooling at the top.
Mitigation: Capsule formulation uses fully-homogenized, stabilized nut butter (added monoglyceride emulsifier at 0.5% by mass, food-safe, standard in commercial nut butter). This is a capsule manufacturing specification requirement, not an appliance-level problem. Alternatively, a "shake before use" instruction on the capsule indicates to the user to invert and press the piston 3 times before loading, remixing the contents.
Residual risk: Low with emulsified formulation.

