## APPENDIX A — FULL PATENT DISCLOSURE TEXT

The following is a complete patent-ready disclosure document suitable as the basis for a provisional utility patent application.

TITLE: Integrated Capsule-Nozzle Food Dispensing and Spreading System for Bread-Toasting Appliances

FIELD OF THE INVENTION

The present invention relates to domestic kitchen appliances and more particularly to toasters and other bread-toasting devices equipped with automated systems for dispensing and spreading viscous spreadable food substances onto the surface of toasted bread.

BACKGROUND

Smart toasters capable of sensor-based browning control and wireless connectivity are known in the art. However, no consumer toaster integrates a system for automatically dispensing and evenly spreading a viscous edible substance — such as jam, nut butter, honey, chocolate spread, or cream cheese — onto bread immediately after toasting.

Previous approaches to incorporating spread-dispensing functionality into toasters have encountered three fundamental barriers:

First: Complex multi-axis robotic arms capable of applying spread with sufficient dexterity are prohibitively expensive for domestic appliances and are mechanically unreliable in the food-contact environment.

Second: Pump-and-permanent-nozzle arrangements suffer severe operational failure because residual spread material dries, crystallizes, or oxidizes within nozzle orifices, tubing, and pump chambers between uses — typically within 24 to 72 hours at ambient kitchen temperature. This clogging requires complete disassembly and manual washing of internal components, representing an unacceptable user burden.

Third: Beverage capsule systems, though well established for liquid products, employ gravity or pressure to drain low-viscosity liquids and are not capable of accurately extruding a highly viscous paste while simultaneously spreading it across a delicate, porous, and geometrically irregular surface such as toasted bread.

There therefore exists a need for an apparatus that reliably dispenses and spreads a viscous food substance onto bread without contaminating the appliance's permanent components, without clogging, and without expensive multi-axis mechanics.

SUMMARY OF THE INVENTION

The invention provides a disposable capsule-nozzle system integrated with a linear carriage drive. The capsule is a single-use, fully sealed unit comprising a reservoir of spreadable food material, a slidable piston, an outlet orifice, and a flexible spreading lip. The carriage picks up a fresh capsule, carries it across the stationary toasted bread along a linear rail, and simultaneously drives the piston forward via a mechanical drive interface on the capsule body. The food substance is thereby extruded through the capsule's own orifice and smoothed into a uniform layer by the spreading lip. Upon completion, the entire capsule is ejected and discarded. No food substance contacts any permanent component of the appliance.

Key inventive aspects of the disclosure are as follows:

First: A disposable capsule comprising an integrated piston, outlet orifice, and flexible spreading lip, wherein the lip profile is matched to the viscosity class of the enclosed spread material.

Second: A linear carriage that both transports the capsule across the bread and advances the capsule's piston via a mechanical coupling, such that piston displacement is geometrically proportional to carriage travel, guaranteeing uniform extrusion volume per unit distance.

Third: An anti-drip mechanism wherein the piston is briefly retracted after dispensing to withdraw food substance from the orifice.

Fourth: A stationary wiper element that cleans the capsule spreading lip during carriage return.

Fifth: A rotary capsule carousel supply system that holds a plurality of capsule types and presents selected capsules to the carriage pick-up station.

Sixth: An RFID identification system wherein each capsule carries a tag readable by the appliance to automatically configure dispensing parameters, identify spread type, and communicate allergen data to the user via a connected mobile application.

Seventh: Integration of the spreading system with a smart toaster having motorized bread lift, AI-based optical browning control, and cloud-connected user personalization.

DETAILED DESCRIPTION

1. Capsule Construction

The capsule body is a rigid cylinder of food-grade polypropylene, 30mm outer diameter and 55mm length. A silicone-rubber piston is slidably disposed within the body, sealing against the inner wall. The product chamber, located between the piston and the outlet end, contains 20mL of spreadable food material. A heat-sealed aluminium foil membrane closes the filling end, providing hermetic containment under a nitrogen-flushed headspace.

The outlet end of the capsule tapers to a PTFE-lined orifice of 2.5mm inner diameter. Surrounding the orifice is a flexible spreading lip of medical-grade liquid silicone rubber. The lip extends beyond the orifice and contacts the bread surface during traversal. The lip geometry is selected according to the viscosity class of the enclosed material. For low-viscosity materials, the lip is a straight thin edge of 2mm extension and 18mm width, designed to minimize surface drag. For medium-viscosity materials containing particulates, the lip carries three longitudinal grooves of 0.8mm width to distribute material laterally during traversal. For high-viscosity pastes, the lip has a tapered wedge profile of 4mm extension and 28mm width, distributing the extrusion force over a larger contact area to prevent bread surface tearing.

A linear gear rack of module 1.0 is molded into the capsule's outer surface, extending 40mm along the longitudinal axis. This rack is the mechanical drive interface that couples the carriage's pinion to the piston. A passive RFID tag operating at 13.56MHz is affixed to the capsule body, storing spread type identifier, viscosity class, allergen flags, manufacturing batch data, expiry date, and recommended operating parameters.

2. Linear Carriage and Piston Drive

The carriage is mounted on an 8mm hardened steel linear rail spanning 200mm across the appliance above the bread slot. A NEMA 8 stepper motor drives a GT2 timing belt to translate the carriage with a step resolution of 0.019mm at 1/16 microstepping.

The carriage body houses a capsule receiver socket, a stainless-steel piercing crown, a spring-loaded clamping collar, a pinion gear of module 1.0 engaging the capsule's rack, and an RC522 RFID reader coil. The piercing crown is fixed in the receiver and penetrates the foil membrane when the capsule is loaded into the receiver. The pinion is coupled to the stepper motor via a bevel gear pair, such that as the carriage translates along the rail, the pinion rotates in engagement with the capsule's rack, advancing the piston forward. The gear ratio is set so that each 1mm of carriage travel produces a 0.11mm piston advance, extruding approximately 0.077mL per millimeter. This mechanical coupling guarantees that extrusion volume is proportional to carriage travel distance without any feedback sensor or flow controller.

The maximum axial force the stepper-pinion system can apply to the piston is approximately 85N. For Class C high-viscosity spreads, the capsule is pre-warmed by a PTC heater at the carousel pick-up station to 35°C before the dispense cycle begins, reducing viscosity to within the motor's force capacity.

3. Anti-Drip and Wiper Systems

Upon completing the dispensing stroke, the stepper motor reverses by a precisely counted step sequence corresponding to 0.15–0.5mm of piston retraction depending on spread viscosity class. This retraction draws food substance back from the orifice, preventing passive dripping during carriage return. The retraction distance is stored per spread type in the capsule's RFID tag and loaded by the control system at cycle start.

A stationary silicone wiper blade, Shore A 30, 3mm thick and 35mm wide, is mounted on the appliance body adjacent to the bread slot. During carriage return to home position, the capsule's spreading lip passes over the wiper, removing residual surface material.

4. Capsule Supply and Ejection

A four-position rotary carousel is mounted on the appliance's side. A 5V micro-servo rotates the carousel under microcontroller command. A secondary fixed RC522 RFID reader at the carousel reads all four capsule slots upon door closure, building an inventory map. When the user selects a spread type, the microcontroller identifies the carousel slot holding that capsule type, commands the servo to index to that position, and then commands the carriage to the pick-up station.

At the end of the spreading cycle, after anti-drip retraction and wiper cleaning, the carriage moves to an ejection position where the clamping collar is electrically released. The spent capsule falls by gravity into a removable stainless steel waste bin. The bin holds up to ten spent capsules.

5. Control Integration

The ESP32-S3 microcontroller coordinates all subsystems. The toasting cycle runs first, controlling heating elements and monitoring the optical browning array via three TCS34725 RGB sensors. The on-device browning predictor (quantized LSTM, TFLite Micro) issues an early-stop signal when the target browning level is detected. The motorized lift then raises the bread to spreading height. The spreading cycle follows the sequence described above. Throughout the cycle, the ACS712 current sensors monitor motor supply rails for stall conditions; the load cell verifies spread delivery by comparing bread weight before and after dispensing.

END OF DETAILED DESCRIPTION

ABSTRACT

A smart toaster system applies spreadable food substances onto toasted bread using single-use disposable capsules each integrating a piston, outlet orifice, and flexible spreading lip. A linear carriage picks up a capsule, traverses it across the bread, and simultaneously advances the piston via a mechanically linked drive, extruding the substance at a rate proportional to carriage travel. The spreading lip smooths the material into a uniform layer. After use, the capsule is ejected into a waste bin, leaving no food residue on any permanent appliance component. This eliminates clogging, requires no user cleaning of the dispensing system, and combines with AI optical browning control and cloud-connected personalization for fully automated breakfast preparation.



